 ## Background
-The Layered Polygonal Area Lights (LPAL) method was proposed by T. Kuge et al. in 2019 [S1]. It was introduced as a real-time technique for indirect illumination of emissive inhomogeneous volumes [S1]. The method builds upon the Linearly Transformed Cosine (LTC) model for surface integration [HDHN16]. It addresses the limitations of Voxel Cone Tracing (VCT) and VPL sampling in the context of glossy reflections from participating media [S1].
+The Layered Polygonal Area Lights (LPAL) method was proposed by T. Kuge et al. in 2019 [S1]. It was introduced as a real-time technique for indirect illumination of emissive inhomogeneous volumes [S1]. The method builds upon the Linearly Transformed Cosine (LTC) model for surface integration [S1]. It addresses the limitations of Voxel Cone Tracing (VCT) and VPL sampling in the context of glossy reflections from participating media [S1].
 
